<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Jülich</h1><p class="st-meta">1003 CE – 1794 CE (approx.) · capital Jülich</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rhineland county turned duchy between Aachen and Cologne, whose union with Cleves and Berg created a powerful state and a famous succession crisis.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A count named Gerhard appears in the Jülichgau around 1003, and his family built a county around the old Roman town of Jülich west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. The counts fought the archbishops of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Cologne</a> for supremacy in the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, sharing the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-worringen">Worringen</a> in 1288 with Berg and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Brabant</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-i-duke-of-julich">Count William V</a> was made margrave in 1336 and duke in 1356. In 1423 Jülich united with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-berg">Duchy of Berg</a>, and in 1521 with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-cleves">Duchy of Cleves</a> and the County of the Mark, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/united-duchies-of-julich-cleves-berg">United Duchies</a>. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">William the Rich</a> built Jülich into an Italian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a> fortress town with a citadel.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When the last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-william-duke-of-julich-cleves-berg">John William</a>, died in 1609, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-julich-succession">Jülich succession crisis</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a> nearly sparked a European war. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-xanten">Treaty of Xanten</a> in 1614 gave Jülich and Berg to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palatinate-neuburg">Palatinate-Neuburg</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> took Jülich in 1794, and in 1815 it became Prussian.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-julich.jpg" alt="Duchy of Jülich" width="320" height="212"><figcaption>Image: ZH2000 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Duchy_of_J%C3%BClich_(1560).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1003 CE – 1794 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Jülich</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>William V of Jülich, William the Rich, John William</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ripuarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>, <a href="/countries/netherlands">Netherlands</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Jülich, Juliers</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=duchy-of-julich" aria-label="Duchy of Jülich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4825 412 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Jülich lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5145 668L5229 694M5145 668L5154 659M5145 668L5259 732M5145 668L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5154 659h0M5259 732h0M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5145 668h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5145 668h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Jülich. <a href="/atlas?year=1398&amp;focus=duchy-of-julich">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

